There's a regression. The "Save edit on Enter" scenario fails:

```gherkin
Scenario: Save edit on Enter
  Given the user has added todo "Buy groceries"
  When the user double-clicks the label for todo "Buy groceries"
  And the user types "Buy milk" into the edit input and presses Enter
  Then a todo "Buy milk" appears in the list
  And todo "Buy milk" is not in editing mode
```

The failure is: `Todo "Buy milk" is still in editing mode`

The issue is in my `render()` function. When I reuse an existing `<li>` element, I need to preserve the editing state. Let me look at the `saveEdit` function:

```js
function saveEdit(li) {
    var id = parseInt(li.getAttribute('data-id'), 10);
    var editInput = li.querySelector('.edit');
    var title = editInput.value.trim();
    if (title === '') {
        removeTodo(id);
    } else {
        var todo = findTodoById(id);
        if (todo) {
            todo.title = title;
            saveTodos(todos);
            render();  // <-- This re-renders the list
        }
    }
}
```

When `saveEdit` is called, it updates the todo's title and then calls `render()`. My `render()` function reuses the existing `<li>` element, but it doesn't remove the `editing` class. So the `<li>` stays in editing mode.

The fix: when reusing an existing `<li>`, I need to also update its `editing` class based on whether the todo is being edited. But the editing state is not stored in the `todos` array — it's only in the DOM.

Actually, the simpler fix is: when reusing an existing `<li>`, remove the `editing` class unless the todo is actively being edited. 

Since `render()` is called after editing completes, the editing state should be cleared. I need to ensure that when updating an existing `<li>`, the `editing` class is removed to reflect that the edit is finished.

